course: dictionary
lesson: 10339
title: Learning a word from its sentences
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A trampoline is a strong, stretchy sheet held tight by springs on a frame, and you jump up and down on it. You may not have one at home, but the sentences around the word give you clues. «A wet trampoline is slippery, so you do not jump on it.» Slippery and do not jump tell you that a trampoline can be dangerous to use in the wrong conditions.
explain: Look for other words that explain or repeat the idea. «On a trampoline or busy playground you take turns and wait until the space is clear.» Take turns and wait until the space is clear show that only one person should be on it at a time.
explain: Always test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. If you think a trampoline is a sort of soft bed, does «Most trampoline injuries happen when several people jump together» still make sense? It does not, because the sentence is about jumping and getting hurt, so the meaning must be the stretchy jumping surface.
fact: A trampoline is a strong, stretchy sheet held tight by springs on a frame.
fact: «Most trampoline injuries happen when several people jump together.» So one person at a time is safer.
fact: «A wet trampoline is slippery, so you do not jump on it.»
fact: Safety rules include taking your shoes off, using a safety net and padded springs, and having an adult nearby.
fact: A trampoline park is a place with many trampolines to bounce on, and the springs stretch and push you back up.
exercise: What is a trampoline? | * A strong, stretchy sheet held tight by springs on a frame, which you jump on | A soft drink in a glass with a straw | A wooden bench in a park | A ladder for climbing up walls
exercise: «A wet trampoline is slippery, so you do not jump on it.» What does slippery mean? | * Likely to make you slide and fall | Very hot in the sun | Full of stretchy springs | Soft and cosy
exercise: «Most trampoline injuries happen when several people jump together.» What is the safest rule this suggests? | Jump on a wet trampoline | Everyone jumps at once | * Take turns, one person at a time | Do somersaults without any training
test: «On a trampoline or busy playground you take turns and wait until the space is clear.» Why do you wait? | Because waiting is always more fun | * So the space is clear and safe for the next person | So you can eat your snack | So the trampoline can dry in the sun
test: Which phrase means a place with lots of trampolines to bounce on? | Bounce on the bench | Padded springs | * Trampoline park | Safety net
exam: Read «Connor pushes Kieran off the trampoline in a row over turns.» What went wrong? | Nobody used the trampoline at all | Everyone shared the turns happily | They went to the trampoline park together | * They had a row over turns, and Kieran got hurt
